messages.content:
{
  "answer": "The FAR states that change orders are generally issued by the contracting officer, unless authority is delegated, and must be in writing on Standard Form 30 unless an urgent electronic order is used; they require appropriate documentation and prompt negotiation of any equitable adjustment.",
  "quotes": [
    {
      "source": "q1",
      "text": "Change orders shall be issued by the contracting officer except when authority is delegated to an administrative contracting officer (see 42.202(c))."
    },
    {
      "source": "q2",
      "text": "(a) Generally, Government contracts contain a changes clause that permits the contracting officer to make unilateral changes, in designated areas, within the general scope of the contract. These are accomplished by issuing written change orders on Standard Form 30, Amendment of Solicitation/Modification of Contract (SF 30), unless otherwise provided (see 43.301)."
    },
    {
      "source": "q2",
      "text": "(c) The contracting officer may issue a change order by electronic means without a SF 30 under unusual or urgent circumstances, provided that the message contains substantially the information required by the SF 30 and immediate action is taken to issue the SF 30."
    },
    {
      "source": "q3",
      "text": "(a) Change order documentation. When change orders are not forward priced, they require two documents: the change order and a supplemental agreement reflecting the resulting equitable adjustment in contract terms."
    },
    {
      "source": "q3",
      "text": "(b) Definitization. (1)(i) Contracting officers shall negotiate equitable adjustments resulting from change orders in the shortest practicable time."
    }
  ]
}

